          <a href="#skip" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. When It Is the Wrong Fix</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">12. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Acoustic caulk is the cheapest part of a soundproofing job and the part most often skipped. It does one thing: it closes the air gaps around drywall, boxes and pipes, and keeps them closed because it never fully hardens. It will not make a thin wall heavy, and no sealant on this page claims otherwise if you read the fine print.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="what-it-does" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Acoustic Caulk Actually Does
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sound gets through a wall two ways. It can shake the wall itself, the drywall, studs and the drywall on the other side, or it can skip the wall entirely and travel through the air in any gap it can find. Acoustic caulk only deals with the second path, and for most rooms that is the path doing the damage. A wall that is acoustically excellent on paper can perform like a cheap partition because the drywall stops a quarter inch above the slab, the outlet boxes are cut oversized, and the top plate gap was never filled.
          </p>

          <p>
            The reason air gaps matter so much is that sound isolation is not an average. A wall with a solid STC 50 assembly and a one percent open area does not perform at 49; it collapses toward the performance of the hole. The Titebond test data in the next section shows that in plain numbers: the same double-layer wall rated STC 29 with open edges and STC 53 with two sealed beads. That 24 point swing came from a few tubes of caulk, not from more drywall.
          </p>

          <p>
            Acoustic caulk differs from ordinary caulk in one property: it is formulated to stay soft. Standard acrylic latex caulk cures to a firm, paintable bead that is fine around trim. Over a few heating seasons it shrinks and hardens, and a hard bead in a joint that moves will crack along one edge. The crack is tiny, but so is the gap you were trying to close. Acoustical sealants are built to remain flexible for the life of the wall, so the bead follows the drywall as the framing expands, contracts and settles. OSI, Titebond and Auralex all describe their products as staying flexible, and that is the property you are paying for.
          </p>

          <p>
            The other thing that separates the category is testing. A sealant sold as acoustical is usually tested to ASTM C919, the standard practice for using sealants in acoustical applications, or tested in a full wall assembly to ASTM E90. Many are also tested for surface burning to ASTM E84 and listed for use in fire-rated joints, which matters because the same perimeter joint that leaks sound often has to stop smoke in a rated wall.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical upshot is simple. If you are building or upgrading a wall for sound, the caulk is the cheapest part of the job and one of the most consequential. If you are not willing to pull outlet covers, find the gap under the baseboard and run a bead at every edge, no acoustic caulk will help you, because the product only works where it is actually applied.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The 5 Best Acoustic Caulks, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            We compared acoustical sealants from OSI, Titebond, Green Glue, Auralex and Liquid Nails on what each maker publishes: base chemistry, whether it is rated to stay flexible, STC test data and the assembly behind it, fire and surface-burning standards, coverage per cartridge and application temperature. We did not run our own sound tests; every figure below comes from a manufacturer data sheet or product page, and where retailers disagree with the maker we go with the maker.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <div id={product.id} class="scroll-mt-24 mt-12 first:mt-8">
              <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-sm hover:shadow-md transition-shadow">
                <div class="bg-gray-900 px-6 py-4 flex items-center justify-between">
