        <div id="not-mass" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Acoustic Caulk Cannot Do: It Is Not Mass
          </h2>

          <p>
            The most common disappointment with acoustic caulk comes from expecting it to do the job of the wall. It cannot. A tube of sealant has almost no mass and adds no damping to a stud wall. If the wall is a single layer of half-inch drywall on each side of a wood stud, sealing every gap gets you the full rating of that wall, which is modest, and no more. You will hear less leakage, especially in the mid and high frequencies where voices live, but you will still hear a television bass line and footsteps.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sound isolation that goes beyond the basic wall comes from three things: more mass, decoupling and damping. Mass means more or heavier drywall. Decoupling means breaking the solid path between the two faces of the wall, with staggered studs, resilient channel installed correctly, or isolation clips and hat channel. Damping means a viscoelastic layer, the job Green Glue Noiseproofing Compound does between two sheets of drywall. Acoustic caulk is the fourth piece that makes the other three show up in the result, but it is never a substitute for any of them.
          </p>

          <p>
            That also means there is no point spraying caulk across a wall face or filling a stud bay with it, both of which come up in forums. The product is a gap filler. A bead about the size the manufacturer specifies, in the joint, is the whole application. Anything more is money that would have bought another sheet of drywall.
          </p>

          <p>
            It is equally important to know what does not count as a gap. Air leaks are the openings that go all the way through: the perimeter of the drywall, the gap where a box penetrates, the hole a pipe or cable passes through, the space around a recessed light or a duct boot. A hairline crack in the face of finished drywall is not an air path unless it goes through. Filling it with acoustic caulk changes nothing, and because the sealant stays soft it can be visible under paint on a smooth wall.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Caulk unlocks the wall's rating. It does not raise it.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Seal every air path in a wall and it performs to its design. To perform better than its design, the wall needs more mass, decoupling or damping, and no sealant provides those.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="stc" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How to Read an STC Claim on a Sealant
          </h2>

          <p>
            STC, sound transmission class, is a single number rating for how well a wall or floor-ceiling assembly reduces airborne sound, measured in a lab to ASTM E90 and rated per ASTM E413. A typical single-stud wall with one layer of half-inch drywall per side lands in the low to mid 30s; double layers, insulation and decoupling push it up into the 50s and beyond. The key word is assembly. STC belongs to a complete wall, not to a material.
          </p>

          <p>
            So when a sealant tube says STC 53, it does not mean the caulk has an STC of 53. It means a specific wall, built and sealed in a specific way, tested at 53. Titebond's published data is the most transparent example. Its ASTM C919 test used double-layer 13 mm wallboard on metal studs. Left uncaulked at the edges, the wall tested at STC 29. With a single perimeter bead under one base layer it reached 49. With two beads, one under each base layer, it reached 53. Auralex quotes the same STC 53 figure for StopGap when it is applied as two beads at the top, bottom and all perimeter edges.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Test condition (Titebond, ASTM C919)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Published STC</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Edges left open</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">29</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">One perimeter bead, one base layer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">49</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Two beads, one under each base layer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">53</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Two lessons come out of that table. First, the sealant brand is not the main variable. Two different products claim the same 53 because the result reflects a well-built double-layer wall that has been sealed properly. Second, the number of beads matters. In a double-layer wall the gap behind the first layer is a separate air path from the gap behind the second, and sealing only the face layer leaves part of the leak in place.
          </p>

          <p>
            Other manufacturers phrase it differently. Liquid Nails says its AS-825 maintains the specified STC value of a wall system and has been tested to ASTM E90. OSI lists SC-175's fire and surface-burning tests rather than an STC chart. Those are not weaker products by default; they are simply making the more accurate claim that a sealant preserves the rating of a wall rather than having one of its own. Be suspicious of any caulk that advertises a big STC number with no wall assembly attached to it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, STC is weighted toward speech frequencies. A wall can post a good STC and still pass low-frequency sound, such as subwoofers and drums, quite well. Caulk does very little for that problem. Bass isolation is a mass and decoupling job, and it is the area where people most often blame the sealant for a limit of the wall.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="where" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Where the Bead Goes
          </h2>

          <p>
            The single most important joint is the bottom of the drywall. Hangers leave a gap of roughly a quarter to half an inch above the floor so the sheet does not wick moisture or bind on an uneven slab, and baseboard covers it. That gap usually runs the full length of every wall, and on a typical room it is the largest open area in the assembly. Run a continuous bead between the drywall edge and the floor or bottom plate before the base goes on. On an existing wall, that means pulling the baseboard, which is a nuisance, but it is also where most of the gain is.
          </p>

          <p>
            The top of the wall and the inside corners come next. Where drywall meets the ceiling, and where two walls meet in a corner, the joint is taped and mudded on the face, but the gap behind the tape is often open to the stud bay. On new work, bead the perimeter of each sheet before the next one is pushed into place, or leave a small gap and fill it with sealant before taping. In a double-layer wall, do it again for the second layer, following the two-bead method from the STC section.
          </p>

          <p>
            Electrical boxes are the classic leak. A box on one side of the wall, cut a little oversized, connected to a box on the other side of the same stud bay, is effectively a hole. Seal the gap between the drywall and every box with acoustic caulk, and seal the knock-outs and cable entries inside the box. Where a box sits back to back with one on the other side, offset them by at least one stud bay on new work. Acoustical putty pads, moldable pads wrapped around the back of the box, are the companion product here; caulk handles the drywall-to-box gap and the pad handles the box itself.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then work through every penetration: pipes, cable bundles, HVAC duct boots, recessed light housings, and the gap around a door frame before the casing goes on. Use the sealant on gaps it can bridge, typically up to about a quarter to three-eighths of an inch. Larger openings want backer rod first, or a mineral wool pack topped with sealant, so the bead has something to sit on and stays the right depth.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Location</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Priority</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Notes</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Drywall bottom edge at floor</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Highest</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Pull baseboard on existing walls</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Ceiling line and inside corners</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">High</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Bead each layer in double-drywall walls</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Outlet and switch boxes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">High</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Add putty pads on the box backs</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Pipes, cables, duct boots</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Medium</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Backer rod for gaps over 3/8 inch</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Door frame rough opening</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Medium</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Before casing; the door itself still leaks</td>
