            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="double-drywall" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Acoustic Caulk in Double-Drywall and Green Glue Walls
          </h2>

          <p>
            The double-drywall wall is the most common real soundproofing upgrade a homeowner does: a second layer of five-eighths inch drywall screwed over the first, often with Green Glue Noiseproofing Compound between them. It adds mass and, with the compound, damping, and it can be done on an existing wall without opening the studs. It is also the job where the caulk matters most, because a heavy wall with open edges wastes the money spent on the second layer.
          </p>

          <p>
            The sequence is straightforward. Seal the perimeter and boxes of the existing layer first. Then apply the compound to the back of each new sheet, hang it with joints offset from the layer beneath, and leave a small gap of about an eighth to a quarter inch at the ceiling, floor and corners. That gap is filled with acoustic caulk, not mud, because a hard joint would rigidly connect the new layer to the adjacent wall or ceiling and short-circuit the damping. Then tape and finish the face as normal. Green Glue's sealant data sheet is explicit that the sealant is not a finishing product and that standard tape and mud go over it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Outlet boxes need box extenders when you add a layer, because the face of the box has to come flush with the new drywall surface. Seal the drywall to the extender just as you sealed it to the original box. This is the step people skip most often on a second-layer job, and it leaves a ring of open air around every outlet on the wall.
          </p>

          <p>
            In walls built on isolation clips and hat channel, the rule is the same with one extra caution: the drywall should not touch the floor, ceiling or adjoining walls anywhere. The perimeter gap is intentional, and acoustic caulk is what fills it without creating a rigid bridge. Squeezing in a hard material, or letting the bottom edge of the sheet sit on the floor, undoes a lot of what the clips are there to do.
          </p>

          <p>
            Any of the sealants on this list will work in these assemblies. Green Glue's sealant earns its spot for people already following that company's instructions, and Titebond and Auralex for publishing two-bead STC results. The bead itself, continuous and at every edge of every layer, is what decides the result.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="chemistry" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Latex Acoustical Sealant vs Non-Curing Butyl
          </h2>

          <p>
            Everything on our list is a water-based acoustical sealant: latex, siliconized acrylic or similar. They go on white, clean up with water while wet, form a skin, and can be painted. That makes them the right choice for most homeowner work, where some of the bead will be visible at a corner or around a box and you want to paint over it.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is a second family worth knowing about: non-skinning, non-hardening synthetic rubber or butyl acoustical sealants. Tremco's Acoustical/Curtainwall Sealant is the best-known example; Tremco describes it as a single-component, non-skinning, non-hardening synthetic rubber sealant for sound-dampening systems, sold in dark gray. DAP makes a comparable gray product, Mono Acoustical Sealant. These never really dry. They stay tacky for years, which is excellent for an air seal in a joint that moves, and they are often specified for curtainwalls and for sealing behind vapor barriers.
          </p>

          <p>
            The tradeoffs are real. Non-curing butyl is messy to apply and stays sticky, so it picks up dust if left exposed, and it is generally not intended to be painted. Tremco's material also contains solvent, so it smells and needs ventilation in a way the water-based products do not. It is mostly sold through commercial distributors in the US; we could not find a listing on Amazon that matched Tremco's part number, which is why it is not one of our five picks.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a homeowner, the decision is simple. Behind a second layer of drywall or under a baseboard where nobody will see or touch it, either type works. Anywhere the bead might be visible or painted, use a water-based acoustical sealant from our list. If a spec or an inspector calls for a particular product in a fire-rated assembly, use exactly that product, because the listing applies to the tested system, not to a category.
          </p>

          <p>
            One more chemistry note: silicone is not the same thing. Pure silicone stays flexible and seals air, but it cannot be painted, it is expensive in the quart sizes, and it is not tested in the acoustical assemblies these products are. It is the right caulk for a tub, not for a party wall.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-regular" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Why Not Use Regular Caulk?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Because it hardens. Ordinary acrylic latex caulk is designed to fill a joint, take paint and stay put, which is exactly what you want at trim, and it is what our guide to the <a href="/best-paintable-caulk/">best paintable caulk</a> is about. Over time it shrinks and loses elasticity. In a sound wall, the perimeter joint moves every time the house cycles through humidity, and a firm bead eventually separates along one edge. The crack may be smaller than a hair, but it is an air path, and air paths are what the product was supposed to remove.
          </p>

          <p>
            Acoustic sealant also tends to come in the 28 oz quart cartridge rather than the 10.1 oz tube, because the volumes involved are large. A single room's perimeter at two layers can be well over 200 linear feet of bead. Buying regular caulk in 10 oz tubes to save a few dollars usually costs more, and the cheapest painter's caulk is the worst performer in exactly the property you need.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is a fair counterpoint. A high-quality elastomeric sealant from a good brand, the kind rated to ASTM C920 for joint movement, will stay flexible for a long time and seal air well. Titebond's acoustical sealant actually meets C920 Class 25 itself. If you are sealing two outlet boxes and already own a tube of a quality flexible sealant, use it. Buy a dedicated acoustical sealant when you are doing a whole wall, a double-drywall job, or anything rated for fire.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are unclear on the vocabulary, our explainers on <a href="/caulk-vs-sealant/">caulk vs sealant</a> and <a href="/silicone-vs-latex-caulk/">silicone vs latex caulk</a> cover the chemistry in more depth. For the outside of the house, where the job is weather rather than sound, the main roundup of the <a href="/best-exterior-caulk/">best exterior caulk</a> is the page to read; acoustic sealants are interior products and do not belong in a siding joint.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, a caution about removal. Because acoustic sealant stays soft, it does not chip out the way old latex does. If you are redoing a joint that was sealed with acoustic caulk, cut the bulk out with a utility knife and clean up the residue. Our roundup of the <a href="/best-caulk-remover/">best caulk remover</a> covers products that soften old beads, and most acoustical sealants respond better to cutting and scraping than to chemistry.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Tubes, Guns and How Much to Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            Almost every acoustical sealant worth buying comes in a 28 oz cartridge, and that size does not fit a standard caulk gun. You need a quart-size gun, sometimes sold as a 29 oz frame. A high-thrust ratio helps, because these sealants are thick and you will be pushing a lot of material; a cheap gun with a weak ratio leaves your hand aching after the first wall. Our guide to the <a href="/best-caulk-gun/">best caulk gun</a> covers the frame sizes and thrust ratios, including cordless options that are worth it on a big job.
          </p>

          <p>
            To estimate quantity, measure the bead length. For a single-layer wall, add up the floor line, ceiling line and both vertical corners for every wall you are sealing, then add about a foot per electrical box and per penetration. For a double-layer wall, double the perimeter figure, because each layer gets its own bead. Then divide by the manufacturer's coverage at the bead size you will actually run. OSI and Titebond both list about 38 feet at a 3/8 inch bead and 86 feet at 1/4 inch; Auralex lists about 32 feet at 3/8 inch.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Published Coverage Per 28 oz Cartridge</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">86 ft</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">OSI / Titebond / Liquid Nails at 1/4 in bead</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">38 ft</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">OSI / Titebond at 3/8 in bead</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">32 ft</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Auralex StopGap at 3/8 in bead</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">40-80 ft</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Green Glue, by bead size</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            As a worked example, a 12 by 14 foot room with an 8 foot ceiling has a perimeter of 52 feet at the floor and 52 at the ceiling, plus four corners at 8 feet each, for 136 feet. Add eight boxes and a few penetrations and call it 150 feet for one layer. At a 3/8 inch bead and 38 feet per tube, that is four tubes. Do both layers of a double-drywall wall and it is eight. That is why the 12-packs on our list are the sensible buy for anything bigger than a closet.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pay attention to temperature and shelf life. Every water-based product here needs about 40 degrees F or warmer to apply, which rules out an unheated garage in January. Green Glue lists a one-year shelf life, and a partly used tube left with the tip open will skin over. Buy for the job in front of you rather than stockpiling.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. When Acoustic Caulk Is the Wrong Fix
          </h2>

          <p>
            If the noise is coming through a door, caulk will not help. A typical hollow-core interior door is the weakest element in most walls, and the gap under it can be larger than every other leak combined. Fix that with a solid-core door, perimeter seals and an automatic door bottom. Sealing the walls around a hollow door is real work with no audible result.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the noise is footsteps from the floor above, the problem is impact sound traveling through the structure, not air through gaps. Caulk at the ceiling line helps a little with airborne sound from above, but footsteps need a decoupled ceiling or a better floor covering upstairs. The same goes for low-frequency music and home theater bass, which moves through mass and framing far more than through gaps.
          </p>

          <p>
            Flanking paths are the other trap. Sound often travels around a wall, through a shared duct run, the joist bay above, an attic, or a continuous subfloor, and lands in the next room without touching the partition you sealed. If you seal a wall carefully and the improvement is smaller than you expected, look at the HVAC returns, the space above the ceiling and any recessed lights before buying more sealant.
          </p>

          <p>
            And if the wall is a single layer of drywall on each side with no insulation, be realistic about the ceiling of what caulk can deliver. Sealing it is still worth doing, because a sealed basic wall beats a leaky one, but most people who want a meaningful drop in voice and television noise end up adding insulation, a second layer of drywall, damping compound or a decoupled system. Plan the caulk as part of that upgrade, not instead of it.
          </p>

          <p>
            The one case where acoustic caulk alone is a great buy is the leaky wall that is otherwise decent: an insulated or double-layer wall with open baseboard gaps and back-to-back outlets. Seal those and the change can be very noticeable, for the cost of a few tubes and an afternoon.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing only the face of the wall</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The tape-and-mud face is not the air seal. The open gap is behind it, at the bottom edge of the drywall, the ceiling line and the corners. In a double-layer wall each layer has its own gap. Bead every edge of every layer, or the wall keeps leaking behind a perfect finish.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the outlet boxes</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Oversized box cut-outs and back-to-back boxes in the same stud bay are some of the biggest leaks in a finished wall. Seal the drywall to every box, seal the knock-outs and cable entries, and add putty pads on the box backs. It is an hour of work per wall and almost always worth it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mudding the perimeter gap in a decoupled wall</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                On clip-and-channel or double-drywall builds, the perimeter gap is there so the new layer does not touch the old walls and ceiling. Filling it with joint compound makes a rigid bridge. Fill it with acoustic sealant, then tape and finish over the top as normal.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting caulk to fix a thin wall</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A sealed single-layer wall is still a single-layer wall. If you want a big drop in voice and TV noise, you need mass, damping or decoupling as well. Budget for the second layer of drywall and use the caulk to make it perform, not in place of it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying 10 oz tubes for a whole room</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 12 by 14 foot room at two layers can need eight 28 oz cartridges. Buying 10 oz tubes means roughly three times as many, more waste and more cost per foot. Get a quart-size gun and buy the case.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does acoustic caulk really work?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, for what it does. It closes air gaps, and air gaps destroy a wall's rating. Titebond's ASTM C919 test shows the same double-layer wall at STC 29 with open edges and STC 53 with two sealed beads. It does not add mass or damping, so it cannot make a basic wall perform like an upgraded one.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between acoustic caulk and regular caulk?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Acoustic sealant is formulated to stay flexible for the life of the wall, so it keeps sealing as the framing moves. Ordinary acrylic latex caulk cures firm and can shrink and crack along an edge. Acoustical products are also usually tested to ASTM C919 or in wall assemblies to ASTM E90, and many carry fire and smoke ratings.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you paint over acoustic caulk?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The water-based sealants on our list, OSI SC-175, Titebond 2892, Green Glue, Auralex StopGap and Liquid Nails AS-825, are all listed as paintable once they have set or dried. Non-curing butyl acoustical sealants such as Tremco's are generally not meant to be painted and stay tacky, so keep those out of sight.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Green Glue sealant the same as Green Glue compound?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. The compound is a damping material applied between two sheets of drywall to turn them into a damped panel. The sealant is a caulk for the edges and penetrations of the wall. A Green Glue wall uses both: compound across the sheet and sealant at the perimeter, boxes and gaps.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much acoustic caulk do I need for a room?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Add the floor line, ceiling line and vertical corners of each wall, plus about a foot per box and penetration, then double it for a double-layer wall. Divide by the coverage at your bead size: about 38 feet per 28 oz tube at 3/8 inch for OSI and Titebond. A typical bedroom at one layer needs about four tubes.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does acoustic caulk stop bass?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not much. Low-frequency sound from subwoofers and drums travels mainly through the mass and framing of the wall, not through small gaps. Sealing still helps the overall result, but bass isolation needs more mass, decoupling with clips and channel, and damping. Caulk is the finishing step in that system, not the solution.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use acoustic caulk outside?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not as a weather seal. The products here are interior acoustical sealants; Green Glue explicitly limits its sealant to interior use. They are not designed for UV, rain and wide outdoor movement. For siding, windows and trim outside, use an exterior-rated sealant from our exterior caulk roundup instead.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

