        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            For most walls, buy OSI SC-175 in the 28 oz size, get a quart-size gun, and put a continuous bead at the bottom edge, ceiling line, corners and every box. If you want to see exactly what that bead is worth, Titebond's two-bead STC 53 result is the best published evidence, and its sealant is an equally good buy.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Then be honest about the wall. Caulk lets a wall perform to its design and nothing more. If the design is a single sheet of drywall on each side, sealing it helps, but the real upgrade is mass, damping or decoupling, with acoustic caulk at every edge to make it count.
          </p>
